<article class="env-lemma" id="0AJQ">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AJQ">Lemma <span data-tag="0AJQ">88.2.2</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a Noetherian ring and let $I \subset A$ be an ideal. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
every object of the category $\mathcal{C}'$ (<a href="/tag/0AL4" data-tag="0AL4">88.2.0.2</a>) is Noetherian, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $B \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}')$ and $J \subset B$ is an ideal, then $B/J$ is an object of $\mathcal{C}'$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for a finite type $A$-algebra $C$ the $I$-adic completion $C^\wedge $ is in $\mathcal{C}'$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
in particular the completion $A[x_1, \ldots , x_ r]^\wedge $ is in $\mathcal{C}'$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Part (4) follows from Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0316" data-tag="0316">10.97.6</a> as $A[x_1, \ldots , x_ r]$ is Noetherian (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00FN" data-tag="00FN">10.31.1</a>). To see (1) by Lemma <a href="/tag/0AJP" data-tag="0AJP">88.2.1</a> we reduce to the case of the completion of the polynomial ring which we just proved. Part (2) follows from Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00MA" data-tag="00MA">10.97.1</a> which tells us that ever finite $B$-module is $IB$-adically complete. Part (3) follows in the same manner as part (4). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>